things like election mobility,

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.660
which I had no idea I was
interested in before.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.250
And so just being exposed to people,
not just the GSB, but

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:05.161
also other Stanford schools, has opened
up my mind to different opportunities

00:09:05.161 --> 00:09:09.220
that I could leverage on the continent
in different industries.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.520
&gt;&gt; I think for me, it's early days, but

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.570
probably one of the biggest differences
I'm noticing is in a lot of the spaces I

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:20.380
was in before, social impact is something
that people think about, you know,

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:23.060
after they've thought about
how much money they make.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:28.210
And I think at Stanford, almost everyone
across the different graduate schools and

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:32.320
the students themselves,
I think people think of social impact

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:37.060
as not something mutually exclusive from
running a commercially viable business, so

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:41.630
I think almost everyone you meet at
Stanford is very conscious about

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.050
not just building a business, but

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:48.270
building a very sustainable business
that is, for the greater good.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:53.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love that, I think also for
me personally coming to Stanford has been

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.640
an amazing challenge in a good way
because we get to sit with 400 incredibly

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:02.530
intelligent people who think very
differently about how different problems

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:06.619
can be solved and your premonition, or

00:10:06.619 --> 00:10:11.190
your previous ideas of how to change the
world are challenged each and every day.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:15.370
And so for me personally, I can definitely
vouch for what these three guys have said

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:20.350
in terms of coming here, thinking I wanted
to change Africa a specific way, and

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:23.730
my specific way was through branding and
through products.

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.540
And then talking to different
people from athletes,

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.890
to people who were coming from
the entertainment industry,

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:33.620
to former entrepreneurs who've been very
successful, and just sitting with them and

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.830
brainstorming with them and
thinking through different problems.

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:38.220
And hearing them ask questions,

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.800
as people who may not have lived on the
continent, are not from the continent, but

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:45.490
are genuinely curious about Africa,
has just been a way for

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:50.170
me to synthesize information, understand
what I actually care about, and know that

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:54.490
it's extremely possible to pursue
whatever it is that you want to pursue.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:57.495
And so that's been something
I loved about the GSB.

00:10:57.495 --> 00:11:00.630
So I'll give one more question, so

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:05.280
you guys and the audience, you guys can
continue to type in your life questions.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.428
I'm already seeing some
questions pop up here.

00:11:07.428 --> 00:11:10.590
And then we'll take some questions
directly from the audience.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:14.492
But the last question I wanted to ask
you guys before we give it over to

00:11:14.492 --> 00:11:15.519
the audience is,

00:11:15.519 --> 00:11:19.496
what have been some of your favourite
activities at the GSB, so far?

00:11:19.496 --> 00:11:22.755
Could be fun, could be academic,
could be sleep.

00:11:22.755 --> 00:11:27.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think for

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:30.870
me, it's been the Africans forum.

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:33.450
The culture that we landed
in was amazing experience.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:37.797
Just showcasing what the content has
to offer to people on campus and also

00:11:37.797 --> 00:11:43.142
interacting with so many business leaders,
academic leaders from the continent either

00:11:43.142 --> 00:11:47.789
focused there or working in the US as
well was just amazing experience for me.

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:51.713
&gt;&gt; You know,
I think not to steal Kev's plan.

00:11:51.713 --> 00:11:55.557
[LAUGH] But I think leading the African
Business Forum with Kev last year was

00:11:55.557 --> 00:11:57.858
definitely a highlight for me.

00:11:57.858 --> 00:12:02.080
And just to add something new,
I think we just have a unique

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:07.250
opportunity to almost be the conduit
between Silicon Valley and Africa.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:12.030
And you find yourself bridging that gap
many times while leading the conference.

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.893
And that was just
an exciting opportunity for

00:12:14.893 --> 00:12:20.015
me to expose business leaders in Africa
to what's happening in Silicon Valley,

00:12:20.015 --> 00:12:25.313
I likewise expose people in Silicon Valley
to what's happening on the continent.

00:12:25.313 --> 00:12:26.501
&gt;&gt; Did you have anything too?

00:12:26.501 --> 00:12:31.086
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think for me what I've
enjoyed most is there's a lot of

00:12:31.086 --> 00:12:36.450
entrepreneurs around the Silicon Valley
that are investing in Africa and

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:41.470
for the African business club to be
able to bring these entrepreneurs

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:47.210
to campus every so often just to speak
to them, that has been quite impactful.

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:52.316
But I think yesterday night was one of
my favorite things on campus to see

00:12:52.316 --> 00:12:57.591
like just the different talent we had
a sort of a bad night where people from

00:12:57.591 --> 00:13:02.696
the business could get to showcase
their talent and I just did not have so

00:13:02.696 --> 00:13:05.019
many classmates who were just-

00:13:05.019 --> 00:13:05.918
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:05.918 --> 00:13:10.741
&gt;&gt; Musically gifted and to even see
Africans get on stage and sing and

00:13:10.741 --> 00:13:13.380
get so much support from the crowd.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.490
That was lovely.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:16.425
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:16.425 --> 00:13:18.920
Yeah, I think you guys
have said everything.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:24.108
Funny enough, one of my favorite
parts of Stanford are the classes.

00:13:24.108 --> 00:13:26.750
I know, [LAUGH] the classes
don't get a lot of love, but

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:31.410
specifically as an MBA too,
I love my classes,

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:36.650
like going to class, we do a lot of
role plays with just titans of industry.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:39.410
Like the person teaching your VC class, or

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:42.860
your venture capital class is somebody who
has been in venture capital for years.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:47.630
The person teaching your real estate class
is the CEO of, what, some of the largest

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:50.180
real estate, or one of the largest real
estate companies in the United States.

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:53.520
So that has just been very humbling for
me.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:57.580
But also, I just can't believe every
single day that I get the opportunity

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:01.160
to learn from such amazingly talented and
accomplished people.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:04.570
So that's one of my favorite
things about the GSB.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:06.770
So now we'll take questions
from the audience.

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:10.532
So I'm going to,
hopefully I pronounce this correctly,

00:14:10.532 --> 00:14:14.560
Borubafadzo Chidau wanted to ask you
a question, Kevin, and the question is,

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:17.550
you mentioned that you were
an entrepreneur before the GSB and

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:21.415
then you mentioned that you interned for
McKenzie and you plan to go back to

00:14:21.415 --> 00:14:25.670
McKinsey, so what is the thinking behind
going from entrepreneurship to consulting,

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:28.720
because usually it's consulting
to entrepreneurship?

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:34.314
So, I started my own company right out of
undergrad and essentially teaching myself

00:14:34.314 --> 00:14:39.590
everything on the job, and coming into
the GSB, I thought I was missing certain

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:44.871
skill sets that I thought large corporate
organisations, superstructured,

00:14:44.871 --> 00:14:49.398
have massive training programs would
be great for me to learn from.

00:14:49.398 --> 00:14:52.438
And also I did choose to go
to consulting at McKinsey.

00:14:52.438 --> 00:14:55.013
I think I did learn quite
a bit over the summer and

00:14:55.013 --> 00:14:59.653
I think there's value in me learning those
things for the next year, year and a half,

00:14:59.653 --> 00:15:03.700
two years, and I think those will
be super valuable in my company.

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:07.994
But also like being brutally honest, if
you look at my resume, if you don't have

00:15:07.994 --> 00:15:12.560
my companies, you will not have an idea of
if they're good or bad companies, right?

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:16.253
And there's a huge theorem put on
working organizations, like McKinsey or

00:15:16.253 --> 00:15:20.250
other organizations, and I think for
me, there's value in doing that.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:23.436
And sometimes people overlook that or
do understate it, but

00:15:23.436 --> 00:15:27.680
I think that's quite important, even on
the continent, so I feel it was a way for

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:29.001
me to get the most for me.

00:15:29.001 --> 00:15:29.970
&gt;&gt; Amazing.

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.891
Thank you.
We have Taban from Johannesburg, and

00:15:32.891 --> 00:15:37.185
he wants to know, how do you go
about finding a mentor at the GSB?

00:15:39.549 --> 00:15:42.970
&gt;&gt; I think that's a good question.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:47.720
I think there are many different
places where you could find a mentor.

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:54.900
Actually, you'd be surprised that a lot of
your classmates could even act as mentors,

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:59.830
because now that I'm thinking actively
about potentially starting something after

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:05.050
business school, maybe in like a one year
horizon after graduating there are so

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:08.100
many founders in my class
who I'm looking to for

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:12.620
mentorship to be able to guide me on
the path of being a first time founder.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:19.290
So that's one avenue, another avenue are
professors as Ruth was saying We're just.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:22.712
It's so humbling to know the kind
of people who teach us classes on

00:16:22.712 --> 00:16:23.530
a daily basis.

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:28.668
And give us access to their network,
give us access to all of their knowledge.

00:16:28.668 --> 00:16:32.893
So I'm taking a class called Startup
Garage where I'm actually working on my

00:16:32.893 --> 00:16:33.482
startup.

00:16:33.482 --> 00:16:38.866
And the caliber of professors in
Startup Garage is just mind-blowing.

00:16:38.866 --> 00:16:41.746
I mean, there's one of my professors,
who I think,

00:16:41.746 --> 00:16:43.860
I'm not sure what the exact figure is.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.928
But has invested in 13 of the unit.

00:16:46.928 --> 00:16:51.185
And this is someone that
comes to class every day and

00:16:51.185 --> 00:16:55.740
is actively helping us
build our business idea.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:58.501
And that's also another avenue for
mentorship.

00:16:58.501 --> 00:17:03.811
Those were the two that came to mind and
[INAUDIBLE] I was there, views.

00:17:03.811 --> 00:17:07.875
&gt;&gt; I can add another avenue if
you're looking for scholarships,

00:17:07.875 --> 00:17:10.725
it's the Knight-Hennessy Scholarship.

00:17:10.725 --> 00:17:11.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:16.308
&gt;&gt; Is a good one to apply to because
I've personally been able to get

00:17:16.308 --> 00:17:17.841
a very good mentor.

00:17:17.841 --> 00:17:19.486
Who's an entrepreneur,

00:17:19.486 --> 00:17:23.753
the scholarship has a large array
of members of the advisory board.

00:17:23.753 --> 00:17:26.801
And many of them come from
very different industries, so

00:17:26.801 --> 00:17:29.156
if you're admitted into the scholarship.

00:17:29.156 --> 00:17:32.187
And there's an advisor who
sort of is interested in

00:17:32.187 --> 00:17:34.591
the same things you're interested in.

00:17:34.591 --> 00:17:38.406
Then they'll reach out and
provide you with mentorship.

00:17:38.406 --> 00:17:40.705
&gt;&gt; I think one more avenue is the alumni.

00:17:40.705 --> 00:17:41.228
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:41.228 --> 00:17:45.354
&gt;&gt; I am pretty close to the person who
interviewed me to come to the JSB.

00:17:45.354 --> 00:17:48.597
And you interact with so
many alumns over your time here and

00:17:48.597 --> 00:17:50.793
you develop some deep relationships.

00:17:50.793 --> 00:17:54.280
And I think that's another avenue you
could get some good mentors here.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:55.562
&gt;&gt; Amazing, thank you guys.

00:17:55.562 --> 00:17:59.304
So we'll have a question from
Warren from South Africa again.

00:17:59.304 --> 00:18:04.175
And Warren wants to know if you found
it relatively easy when you entered

00:18:04.175 --> 00:18:06.461
your internship for the summer.

00:18:06.461 --> 00:18:08.583
So this is a question for and Kevin.

00:18:08.583 --> 00:18:10.921
And so when you entered your
internship for the summer,

00:18:10.921 --> 00:18:13.322
did you find it relatively
easy to get your first choice?

00:18:13.322 --> 00:18:20.802
Or did you feel like you had to settle for
your second, third, maybe fourth choice.

00:18:20.802 --> 00:18:25.717
&gt;&gt; Okay, I can go, and I would say
that my route for my internship,

00:18:25.717 --> 00:18:29.828
I probably wouldn't recommend
in hindsight [LAUGH].

00:18:29.828 --> 00:18:32.524
But I knew that I really,
really wanted to work for X.

00:18:32.524 --> 00:18:35.148
I really wanted to work specifically for

00:18:35.148 --> 00:18:38.323
one of the graduates at
X projects called Learn.

00:18:38.323 --> 00:18:42.483
So essentially,
I put all of my eggs in one basket, and

00:18:42.483 --> 00:18:45.655
I would not recommend that in hindsight.

00:18:45.655 --> 00:18:46.515
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But

00:18:46.515 --> 00:18:52.120
I just felt that my profile, I'd worked
in consulting, parts, business school.

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.282
I didn't have a profile
that might stand out to.

00:18:55.282 --> 00:19:00.154
And I felt that I needed to put it in
a lot of work to prove that I wanted to

00:19:00.154 --> 00:19:01.082
work there.

00:19:01.082 --> 00:19:04.679
So what that meant for me was I
spent three months researching and

00:19:04.679 --> 00:19:07.423
talking to people in
the aerospace department.

00:19:07.423 --> 00:19:10.943
because it was space technology.

00:19:10.943 --> 00:19:14.594
Immersing myself in space tech, and

00:19:14.594 --> 00:19:19.196
I then did an outside game strategy for
Learn.

00:19:19.196 --> 00:19:22.156
And all of that took a three-month period,

00:19:22.156 --> 00:19:26.078
what that meant is I couldn't
apply to any other company.

00:19:26.078 --> 00:19:29.063
So I'm just thinking in hindsight,
if it hadn't worked out.

00:19:29.063 --> 00:19:32.001
I probably wouldn't have had
an internship over the summer,

00:19:32.001 --> 00:19:34.465
so I don't recommend it,
but that was my journey.

00:19:34.465 --> 00:19:38.138
&gt;&gt; But having privy to some of
your experiences last year,

00:19:38.138 --> 00:19:40.528
also have worked extremely hard, guys.

00:19:40.528 --> 00:19:43.460
She didn't just put it in the basket and
just let it sit there.

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:48.290
because I also know that you took computer
science classes which takes a lot of time,

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:51.185
specifically for
the schedule of the MBA student.

00:19:51.185 --> 00:19:54.820
So you definitely put
the eggs in one basket, but

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:59.887
you made sure that your resume was
very strong by the end of the year.

00:19:59.887 --> 00:20:04.772
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, that's true, because
I knew I was only doing one company,

00:20:04.772 --> 00:20:06.720
I literally gave it my all.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.037
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And I wanted my application to be

00:20:09.037 --> 00:20:12.740
an application that would
at least get a call back.

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:14.010
Yeah, so it kind of worked out.

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:16.720
&gt;&gt; I might at the extreme
end of that spectrum,

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:20.410
where I came in with different
ideas of what I wanted to explore.

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:22.618
Consulting was definitely
very close to the top,

00:20:22.618 --> 00:20:26.780
I flirted with the idea of investment
banking, venture capital, tech.

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:30.994
And I thought about applying to all these
these industries and all these companies.

00:20:30.994 --> 00:20:34.590
And you're going to probably realize
like you can't devote as much time and

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:36.045
effort to apply to all these.

00:20:36.045 --> 00:20:38.895
And your application will definitely
not stand out if you spreading

00:20:38.895 --> 00:20:39.748
yourself too thin.

00:20:39.748 --> 00:20:44.310
And so I focused on Consulting for
the internship, and yeah, I got McKinsey.

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:48.274
So I'm glad I did,
it was a great experience.

00:20:48.274 --> 00:20:49.190
&gt;&gt; Amazing, so

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:53.340
we have a question from David which
I'm guessing is from Nigeria [LAUGH].

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:56.700
But the question says, asides from Paga,
can you please talk about

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.886
other startups that have been
founded by African Stanford alumn.

00:20:59.886 --> 00:21:04.860
And can you spotlight some PE firms
in Africa that are doing some

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:07.280
interesting things to
develop the continent.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:11.350
So Eugene, let you take the PE question,
and then after that, Kevin,

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:13.420
maybe you guys can talk about startups.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:17.831
And running companies that you
know have been founded by alumn.

00:21:17.831 --> 00:21:23.666
&gt;&gt; Okay, besides Paga I
think this NALA in Tanzania,

00:21:23.666 --> 00:21:28.833
there is Savannah Fund
which is a VC in Kenya.

00:21:28.833 --> 00:21:31.822
Zipline in Uganda,
I'm not sure if it's GSB alumns.

00:21:31.822 --> 00:21:36.153
&gt;&gt; I think Stanford alumns-
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]

00:21:36.153 --> 00:21:37.240
&gt;&gt; In addition,

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:42.224
like from the social impact perspective,
the entire ALA group so

00:21:42.224 --> 00:21:47.221
African Leadership Academy,
African Leadership University.

00:21:47.221 --> 00:21:51.072
&gt;&gt; All of this started
up by Stanford alumn.

00:21:51.072 --> 00:21:52.480
&gt;&gt; Then I can add two,

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:57.955
there's a solar company called
d.lights [INAUDIBLE] GSB alumn.

00:21:57.955 --> 00:22:01.960
And then there's a pioneer high schools.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:04.047
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes.

00:22:04.047 --> 00:22:05.122
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:05.122 --> 00:22:08.991
&gt;&gt; Picky [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:22:08.991 --> 00:22:14.201
maybe I can mention VC as well,
so I think Kev already

00:22:14.201 --> 00:22:19.540
mentioned Savannah Fund
[INAUDIBLE] out of Tanzania.

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:26.458
[INAUDIBLE] that's an alumn and he's
focusing a lot on local entrepreneurs.

00:22:26.458 --> 00:22:33.330
Which is a segment which is pretty much
ignored by the much of the VC funds.

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:38.100
And the other VC fund I can
mention is TL Cohen Capital

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:43.110
which has three GSB
alumns as partners there.

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:44.889
I think on the PE side,

00:22:44.889 --> 00:22:50.993
it's a sector that there was a lot of
excitement in the mid to late 2000s.

00:22:50.993 --> 00:22:53.501
And that has pretty much dipped now that

00:22:53.501 --> 00:22:56.550
the developed markets
are performing better.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:23:01.000
So there's a bit of negative
feeling there at the moment.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:04.440
But if I think about it, there are still
the funds that are doing a good job.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:06.904
It's pretty much the same names.

00:23:06.904 --> 00:23:10.568
But I think
Development Partners International,

00:23:10.568 --> 00:23:15.177
[INAUDIBLE] Of London has been
the best performing fund overall.

00:23:15.177 --> 00:23:20.653
And maybe Helios is probably
the bigger brand [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:20.653 --> 00:23:27.772
performance they should have led,
Helios, DPI, you can't go wrong.

00:23:27.772 --> 00:23:32.826
I'm also looking at a couple
of funds like in London,

00:23:32.826 --> 00:23:38.085
and ITO's out of South Africa that
met a couple of African MBAs.

00:23:38.085 --> 00:23:43.444
Been turned down the other way
have a very good opinion of it.

00:23:43.444 --> 00:23:45.137
And maybe just to go to the other end,

00:23:45.137 --> 00:23:47.222
I think everyone looks
at the very big funds.

00:23:47.222 --> 00:23:52.622
But there are some smaller funds
that have very good teams,

00:23:52.622 --> 00:23:56.087
one of them run by GSB alumni.

00:23:56.087 --> 00:24:02.134
It's a pretty decent team, so maybe not
that big in size, but a very decent team.

00:24:02.134 --> 00:24:07.473
And playing in a very necessary
strategist of grid power.

00:24:07.473 --> 00:24:10.303
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; I think one was Africa 15?

00:24:10.303 --> 00:24:14.366
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Casablanca, I think.

00:24:14.366 --> 00:24:17.024
&gt;&gt; And I think the cool thing Going
quickly back to the company that

00:24:17.024 --> 00:24:18.000
had been started by.

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:23.450
I think most or all of us here have had
the opportunity to interact with at least

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:25.970
two or three of these founders.

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:29.960
And I think coming to Stanford and
now understanding what leadership training

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:34.072
looks like, it's very clear that these
people were very positively impacted by

00:24:34.072 --> 00:24:36.804
the leadership training that
you do get at Stanford.

00:24:36.804 --> 00:24:42.656
Because the leadership style speaks to
a leader that you feel cares about you and

00:24:42.656 --> 00:24:44.578
cares about the impacts,

00:24:44.578 --> 00:24:49.440
just as much as they care about
building a successful company.

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:52.380
That's been my personal
experience with these alumni,

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:54.900
which was going back
to the first question,

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:58.350
one of the biggest reasons why I
personally chose to come to Stanford.

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:02.750
So we're going to take a question
from Kareem from Egypt.

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:07.010
So Kareem wants to travel to the United
States to visit Stanford University and

00:25:07.010 --> 00:25:09.640
learn more about the MBA program and
the Stanford culture.

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:13.800
And his question is,
when would you recommend that he visit?

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:16.990
I would say that during the African
business forum clearly [LAUGH].

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:20.170
I don't know if you guys
have other answers.

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:21.240
&gt;&gt; It's also during spring, right?

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:22.230
&gt;&gt; Is also doing spring.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:23.805
It's a beautiful time of year.

00:25:23.805 --> 00:25:28.246
We'll give you more details on the African
Business Forum before this video is over,

00:25:28.246 --> 00:25:32.194
but that's personally the best time to
come to Stanford, because you really get

00:25:32.194 --> 00:25:35.852
to see how much the school has a play,
and has a stake on the continent.

00:25:35.852 --> 00:25:38.110
You get to interact with a lot
of African students here.

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:40.442
You get to meet a lot
of amazing professors.

00:25:40.442 --> 00:25:44.240
Spring is the time where people
are just generally more relaxed and

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:47.890
their classes are just more fun.

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:51.600
The sun's out, so yes, spring is
definitely the best time to come.

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:54.554
Anything else before I move on?

00:25:54.554 --> 00:25:55.878
&gt;&gt; I have something to add.

00:25:55.878 --> 00:26:00.255
Also, I don't want to feel like you should
come to see the campus before you apply.

00:26:00.255 --> 00:26:01.179
&gt;&gt; Very good [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:01.179 --> 00:26:04.571
&gt;&gt; I never came here to see the GSB and
it was a great decision nonetheless, so

00:26:04.571 --> 00:26:07.435
don't feel obliged to come to
visit the campus if you can't.

00:26:07.435 --> 00:26:08.295
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a good point.

00:26:08.295 --> 00:26:11.168
&gt;&gt; If you're in the area,
if you want to come anyway, feel free,

00:26:11.168 --> 00:26:14.922
that's a good decision, but
don't feel obliged to make the trip.

00:26:14.922 --> 00:26:18.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's such a great point,
because it doesn't do anything for

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.822
your application to come or
not to come, zero.

00:26:20.822 --> 00:26:23.930
Your application to come or not to come
to the campus, so great point, Kevin.

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:30.490
So let's talk about this
question about Silicon Valley.

00:26:30.490 --> 00:26:34.090
So Sadiq Goma wants to know
how involved are you guys,

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:39.010
if at all with Silicon Valley, and has it
been easy to promote African solutions and

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:41.870
raise funding, if you've tried or
if you know anybody who's tried?

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:50.250
&gt;&gt; I think, Silicon Valley, before I
came to Stanford, could feel like a very

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:54.860
nebulous concept of, what exactly
is Silicon Valley, where is it?

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.870
Is there a location, is there-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:26:57.870 --> 00:26:59.810
&gt;&gt; An area of land, Silicon Valley?

00:26:59.810 --> 00:27:03.703
I think coming to Stanford,
I realized that Stanford and

00:27:03.703 --> 00:27:07.120
Silicon Valley,
kind of you can't separate it.

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:10.980
Stanford is so embedded in Silicon Valley.

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:15.583
You're constantly exposed to
entrepreneurs, you're constantly exposed

00:27:15.583 --> 00:27:20.200
to VC investors, all of the things
that makes Silicon Valley what it is.

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:25.400
Now the second question around raising
money as an African entrepreneur,

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:26.810
I personally haven't done it.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:30.420
I know Kevin maybe can talk
a little bit more about this.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:35.073
But I've spoken to African entrepreneurs
and there have been people that have been

00:27:35.073 --> 00:27:39.128
very successful, like Kobo360
just raised a $30 million round,

00:27:39.128 --> 00:27:43.760
people that are raising money I know that
sometimes it takes a little longer and

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:46.540
sometimes you need to
show a bit more traction.

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:51.080
But I'm encouraged by the fact that
quite a few people have done it and

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:54.020
it just seems to be on the rise here,
yeah.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:59.387
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I also have been trying to
raise here, I'm not actively exploring

00:27:59.387 --> 00:28:05.580
[INAUDIBLE], but I do know Africa and
raised funds whilst in school.

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:10.303
And one that comes to my mind is Nala,
who joined YC right after the GSB and

00:28:10.303 --> 00:28:12.250
was able to raise some funds.

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:14.480
So it is doable.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:18.508
I don't know if there's any Africans
in our class who've tried, or

00:28:18.508 --> 00:28:20.690
even in the class above ours.

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:23.638
But looking broader at
the class in general,

00:28:23.638 --> 00:28:28.800
there's a lot of people who have
raised funds for their companies.

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:30.565
So it's not.

00:28:30.565 --> 00:28:33.140
&gt;&gt; I can also think
the person who probably

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:36.420
mentored me a lot when I was
applying to Stanford is from Kenya.

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:40.060
And I mean, I can't mention the company
because he is yet to officially launch it,

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:43.692
but I know he developed
the idea when he was here, and

00:28:43.692 --> 00:28:48.310
the first investors in the fund
were a couple of his professors.

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:51.444
A lot of the Stanford professors
are venture capital investors and

00:28:51.444 --> 00:28:54.090
they're always looking for
great ideas from students.

00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:56.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
I also think just building off of that,

00:28:56.180 --> 00:28:59.580
Silicon Valley doesn't
also just look like VCs.

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:03.110
It looks like going to big tech and
working on emerging markets,

00:29:03.110 --> 00:29:03.793
work within there.

00:29:03.793 --> 00:29:07.360
I know, Lamiday worked at Luna,
I was at Google this summer.

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:11.130
And I was so immersed in all of
the different opportunities that Google

00:29:11.130 --> 00:29:15.930
provides any employee who is interested
in pursuing some sort of initiative on

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:19.840
the continent, whether that's taking
a six-month externship just to pursue your

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:22.230
own idea within Google,
that's something that the company does.

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:25.190
I think other big tech companies do that.

00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:30.891
Also, speaking of Nala and also speaking
of some Class of 2018 graduates

00:29:30.891 --> 00:29:37.170
that I know, who also founded a company
in Kenya called Safi Analytics.

00:29:37.170 --> 00:29:41.930
Silicon Valley can also look like some
of your friends and classmates who

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:46.930
are very interested in supporting what
you're doing both from a mentorship,

00:29:46.930 --> 00:29:51.530
partnership perspective but sometimes
also from a financial perspective, too.

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:56.300
So, we'll take probably one more question,
and I love this question.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:59.200
So this is from Ojoso Ankara, and

00:29:59.200 --> 00:30:03.630
the question says, what would you say
stood out most in your application?

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:06.860
And what advice would you
give future applicants?

00:30:06.860 --> 00:30:10.760
And before these three lovely people
answer, I would just like to preface

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:15.280
everything they say by,
everybody's application is very different.

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:19.190
You do not need to feel like you need to
copy somebody's specific application to

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:24.780
get there or that their application is the
best recipe for how to get to Stanford.

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:28.190
But I do think that everybody's story
matters and it helps you to kind of

00:30:28.190 --> 00:30:33.000
understand people's experiences,
what they felt mattered to them

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:36.452
as they were going through
the process of applying to the GSB.

00:30:36.452 --> 00:30:41.440
&gt;&gt; Thank you, so I think,
to me, I'm not 100% sure what

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:45.550
stood out in my application, but
if I was to reflect a little bit,

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:51.720
I think what matters to you the most
actually is a key part of the application.

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:56.260
And so how I thought of my application
is a portfolio of different things.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:31:00.820
I used my resume,
used my recommendations to almost

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:03.892
tell the story of the achievements
that I had had thus far.

00:31:03.892 --> 00:31:08.000
And then what matters to me, or
what matters to you the most, actually I

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:12.750
used it as an opportunity to just tell the
story of Lamide behind the achievements.

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:20.730
And it was just a very, I think,
very real raw essay about what matters to

00:31:20.730 --> 00:31:26.230
me the most without judgment, just stating
it as it is, not trying to impress.

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:30.820
And I think that might have
been an ingredient, but

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:33.270
I would never know what
exactly got me here.

00:31:34.708 --> 00:31:39.500
Yeah, I mean, it's really hard to know
unless you sit in that what they liked

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:44.020
about your application, but maybe if
I speak to what I think is different

00:31:45.110 --> 00:31:47.595
in my profile and everyone has a map.

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:53.610
So I think most Africans have been to
only their country on the continent,

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:58.470
that's one thing, that most Africans
haven't traveled a lot in Africa.

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:01.330
And I had two jobs that
allowed me to travel so

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:02.860
much in many other African countries,

00:32:02.860 --> 00:32:07.660
I think probably worked in almost 16
African countries on different projects.

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:13.300
And I think the depth at which I was able
to speak about African issues, I think,

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:17.520
is probably different from some others,
maybe just lived in one part of Africa.

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:21.840
I think that's something that came
out in my application Thinking,

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:25.910
I can't point to what I thought was
the most important part of my application,

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:27.200
from the school's perspective.

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:27.709
So I can't see what got me in.

00:32:27.709 --> 00:32:32.788
But looking at it from my perspective,
at what I put in the most work in,

00:32:32.788 --> 00:32:36.480
I think was the one with
the most essays will.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:37.720
And why I say that is,

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:42.080
when I was have different versions of
essay at some point I did everything.

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:45.640
I started typing from
scratch super raw essay.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:49.160
And I say tearing up when I was
typing it like yeah, this is me.

00:32:49.160 --> 00:32:51.100
But I don't know if that's
what brought me in or not.

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:52.560
It might be something else.

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:56.390
But to me, that's what I felt was most
important for me in the application.

00:32:56.390 --> 00:32:57.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:57.070 --> 00:33:00.066
&gt;&gt; For me it was just my raw
intelligence and beauty.

00:33:00.066 --> 00:33:02.035
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Just kidding.

00:33:02.035 --> 00:33:05.708
[LAUGH] No, is it that funny?

00:33:05.708 --> 00:33:11.302
[LAUGH] No but I think similar to what
people are saying here I think our

00:33:11.302 --> 00:33:18.520
applications made sense in terms of where
we're coming from and what we want to do.

00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:23.820
And it probably showed a lot that
we thought really intentionally

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:29.290
about what we want to do with
a business degree at Stanford, and

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:31.320
why we want to do those things.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:34.870
And so I think the biggest thing that I'm
getting from this panel is just the idea

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:36.250
of authenticity and

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:40.340
not feeling like you need to be somebody
different to get into Stanford.

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:43.130
Everybody talks about writing real and
raw essays and talking about your

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:48.468
actual experiences and that was
definitely my, my experience as well.

00:33:48.468 --> 00:33:51.793
So we're going to take one more
sort of general question and

00:33:51.793 --> 00:33:53.880
then we'll sort of close this out.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:59.790
So we are getting a lot of questions about
what financially looks like at the GSB.

00:33:59.790 --> 00:34:04.590
So I'll just kind of cap an umbrella
everything by saying, if you don't already

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:09.460
know the GSB offers need based fellowships
and not necessarily not merit merit based

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:12.920
fellowships, but the need based
fellowships are very generous and

00:34:12.920 --> 00:34:18.100
are based on sort of an objectively
scaled system that the GSB runs.

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:20.810
Also there are other ways to find your

00:34:20.810 --> 00:34:23.480
MBA program like the knight
Hennessy scholarship.

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:27.060
I know, for example, in Nigeria,
there are different organisations and

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:30.580
corporations that offer different
types of full scholarships.

00:34:30.580 --> 00:34:34.160
I'm imagining that some may exist on
other countries on the continent, but

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:38.310
I would definitely say that the biggest,
one of the one of the best things I did

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:43.380
was having a mind switch and thinking
about the GSB from a debt perspective and

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.380
more from an investment perspective.

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:48.540
And I think that that just
helps you to ease your mind

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:53.720
because what you get by mere
default of being on this campus

00:34:53.720 --> 00:34:59.130
is a huge investment that yield
opportunities for you for years to come.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:03.130
But you can definitely
go on gsb.stanford.edu

00:35:03.130 --> 00:35:07.470
to find out more about what
financial aid looks like for

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:13.950
you as an individual planning to apply for
next year, or the years to come.

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:15.550
So the last questions.

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:18.200
Our last group of questions I'm
getting actually have less to do with

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:19.420
student life.

00:35:19.420 --> 00:35:22.150
People want to know what
student life is like culture.

00:35:22.150 --> 00:35:25.030
What do you do on a typical
Thursday Friday, so

00:35:25.030 --> 00:35:29.120
just, rapidly let's go about and
talk about maybe what your,

00:35:30.410 --> 00:35:33.220
favorite pastime is or
something that's really fun for you.

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:34.990
I think we've talked about
a lot of serious things so

00:35:34.990 --> 00:35:36.906
people want to know if you have fun or
not.

00:35:36.906 --> 00:35:41.400
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we have a lot of fun [LAUGH]

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:46.390
which is difficult since
the schedule is always super packed.

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:53.140
You mentioned like Thursday to me that
Thursday is my busiest day at 8am to 9pm

00:35:53.140 --> 00:36:00.130
for classes and then there's beer
only after that, interesting.

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:02.160
So there's time to everything.

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:03.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
&gt;&gt; But I'm also a lot more introverted.

00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:06.780
So I take time out to spend
time with myself like

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:10.280
watching shows just reflecting on
something that is looked at to happen.

00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:15.383
I mean, it is business school,

00:36:15.383 --> 00:36:21.165
there is always something [CROSSTALK]
how do you spread yourself across.

00:36:21.165 --> 00:36:25.829
I probably had to make it to four
different events after 7 pm [CROSSTALK]

00:36:25.829 --> 00:36:30.334
yesterday, I think the good thing
with Stanford if most of the first

00:36:30.334 --> 00:36:35.096
year students leave right next to
the campus and to resume over the GSB.

00:36:35.096 --> 00:36:38.120
So it's you get really
know each other and,

00:36:38.120 --> 00:36:43.180
a lot of the things that are happening
are really close to you.

00:36:43.180 --> 00:36:46.340
And I think that's the beauty
of being in California Ivy Tech,

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:48.380
one or two hour drive.

00:36:48.380 --> 00:36:51.100
In whichever direction, there's a beach or

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:54.990
a mountain, people are always
planning those kinds of things.

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:57.835
And so that makes it really,
really really fun.

00:36:57.835 --> 00:36:58.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:58.400 --> 00:37:01.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think just add
to what these guys have said.

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:03.255
I think one thing that comes to mind for

00:37:03.255 --> 00:37:07.115
me, I love the fact that in Stanford
you can really be who you are.

00:37:07.115 --> 00:37:08.255
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:37:08.255 --> 00:37:12.575
&gt;&gt; There's some people that love to
go to BPL every week or go to which

00:37:12.575 --> 00:37:17.562
is like a huge party, and that's fine and
there's a community around that.

00:37:17.562 --> 00:37:20.200
There are people like me who
are a little bit more introverted.

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:22.510
I like having people over for dinner or

00:37:22.510 --> 00:37:26.790
watching movies with people in my house,
and that's perfectly fine too.

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:33.360
And there's no pressure to be either or
both are accepted and supported.

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:34.740
So that's what I've loved.

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:38.200
&gt;&gt; One thing for
me that I'll highlight is traveling.

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:39.250
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:43.660
&gt;&gt; It's been such a fun experience
traveling with different classmates.

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:48.294
And I think some maybe a stereotype
about business schools is that you're

00:37:48.294 --> 00:37:52.760
traveling to the amazing places in Asia,
Australia and all these places.

00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:56.432
But people have very interesting
travel trips that they do.

00:37:56.432 --> 00:37:59.905
Whether that's we're all going to
travel to Jordan to volunteer for

00:37:59.905 --> 00:38:02.140
not for profit organization together.

00:38:02.140 --> 00:38:05.261
Or we're going to travel to Cuba,
which was a really fun job.

00:38:05.261 --> 00:38:10.827
Or we're going to go Go to New Zealand and
do really amazing things.

00:38:10.827 --> 00:38:13.976
Or we're going to go to Austin,
Texas for South by Southwest,

00:38:13.976 --> 00:38:17.490
which is a huge music festival
that happens every year.

00:38:17.490 --> 00:38:20.800
People just find different
things that interest them.

00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:22.650
Find groups of people
with similar interests.

00:38:22.650 --> 00:38:25.500
Or people that are just
interested in traveling with you.

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:29.500
And that has been such an amazing fun
experience to really get off campus,

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.220
get out of the state sometimes.

00:38:31.220 --> 00:38:33.860
Get to know people in
a very different space.

00:38:33.860 --> 00:38:36.560
And really get to build what I would think

00:38:36.560 --> 00:38:40.150
are some of the strongest bonds that
we can build with out classmates.

00:38:40.150 --> 00:38:42.290
Not being in our element.

00:38:42.290 --> 00:38:43.800
And fun is what you make it.

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:47.940
I think you can be in Vegas and not
have fun because you're a boring person.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:50.750
Or you can be in your home
on a Saturday night and

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:53.720
having the best of fun because
you just make it that way.

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:57.500
So I would say that business
school in general is definitely

00:38:57.500 --> 00:39:00.230
a place where you can have
as much fun as you want.

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:03.130
And still be as focused as you want.

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:06.085
And we can provide a little
bit more detail later.

00:39:06.085 --> 00:39:09.220
[LAUGH].
As you guys start to apply.

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:13.830
So, I just wanted to make one
last comment, which is that

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:17.340
a lot of people want to know how they
can connect with African business club.

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:21.860
So you can connect with African business
club through the gsb.stanford.edu.

00:39:21.860 --> 00:39:24.410
And like all of these
guys have mentioned and

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:27.740
I've also mentioned the Africa
Business Forum is really an event

00:39:27.740 --> 00:39:32.280
that helps you to connect with us on
a really fun weekend in the spring.

00:39:32.280 --> 00:39:37.260
It's happening April 11 this year, and
we're super excited because this forum

00:39:37.260 --> 00:39:43.250
brings around some of the most intelligent
minds, thought partners, policy makers,

00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:48.550
business moguls from the continent
to really come in and inspire and

00:39:48.550 --> 00:39:53.220
challenge us here in the Bay Area,
in the center of Silicon Valley.

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:57.640
So we're super excited for the conference
to be held again in the spring this year,

00:39:57.640 --> 00:40:02.370
so as we sort of come around to a close,
I wanted everybody just to go around and

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:07.220
give your words of wisdom to all of
the amazing people listening right now as

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:09.570
they're getting ready
to apply to Stanford.

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:13.460
It could be about anything you want
it to be, but what would you tell

00:40:13.460 --> 00:40:18.490
hundreds of our listeners right now about
student life, applying to Stanford.

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:20.790
The best advice you can
give them anything.

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:22.610
I can start.

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:27.340
So I think one thing that

00:40:27.340 --> 00:40:32.160
I did a lot was, even before
applying to Stanford, there was so

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:34.060
many times when I thought
I would never get in.

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:38.060
And I think a lot of people
don't even end up applying.

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:41.660
I know you guys on the call,
which is a step in the right direction.

00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:45.580
But a lot of people ask us, these calls
I felt the same way, I'm just like,

00:40:45.580 --> 00:40:47.840
I'm never going to get in.

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:54.230
And I find that African, not to generalize
Nigerian, specifically which I know.

00:40:54.230 --> 00:40:56.700
People I've spoken to just
opt out to the process.

00:40:57.830 --> 00:41:02.950
And think that I don't have the profile,
I haven't done anything amazing,

00:41:02.950 --> 00:41:06.900
but I would just encourage
you to believe in yourself.

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:11.810
Apply, put your best foot forward,
reach out to any of us if you need help.

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:16.250
because we're all about getting
more Africans to come to GSP.

00:41:16.250 --> 00:41:20.294
So, reach out to any of us if you need
help, please don't opt out by yourself.

00:41:23.602 --> 00:41:27.685
&gt;&gt; I think when I say that there's
a lot of reasons not to apply.

00:41:27.685 --> 00:41:29.075
And if you're going to look for

00:41:29.075 --> 00:41:31.315
those reasons not to apply,
you'll find a lot of them.

00:41:31.315 --> 00:41:33.395
And I did find a lot of them.

00:41:33.395 --> 00:41:36.805
I remember one time,
reading just a list of pros and

00:41:36.805 --> 00:41:41.530
cons of some probably that were not
even invited to interview at Stanford.

00:41:41.530 --> 00:41:45.070
And I thought, what business do I
have applying in the first place?

00:41:45.070 --> 00:41:49.230
So I think people are really different.

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:54.600
There's greatness in everyone, so
I think I just do some soul searching and

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:59.380
find that those that strength that you
haven't voted to bring to Stanford.

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:01.090
And focus on that and I'd say for

00:42:01.090 --> 00:42:04.490
people worrying about am I
likely be able to afford it.

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:10.410
I'm saying focus on getting in and
you'll be surprised at how much,

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:15.020
how hard schools will work to make sure
you can afford it after you get admitted.

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:16.695
&gt;&gt; So, that was my first point.

00:42:16.695 --> 00:42:21.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My second one is If you're applying,

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:22.060
just be yourself.

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:23.220
Just be super authentic.

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:25.150
Don't try to be someone else.

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:29.070
As we've mentioned, everyone has
value they bring to this community.

00:42:29.070 --> 00:42:32.630
Don't be afraid if you don't have
a background everyone else has.

00:42:32.630 --> 00:42:35.220
Be comfortable in what you've done and
there's a place for it as well.

00:42:36.600 --> 00:42:38.320
I echo everything they said.

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:42.120
So I just want to thank our
panelists Eugene, and Kevin for

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:45.350
taking time out of their
schedule to speak to you guys.

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:48.890
Feel free to reach out to
us at GSB.stanford.edu.

00:42:48.890 --> 00:42:51.000
And we appreciate you guys so much.

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:53.170
We can't wait to see a lot of you guys,

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:56.910
a lot more of you guys in
the coming years at the GSB.

00:42:56.910 --> 00:42:57.705
So thank you all very much.

00:42:57.705 --> 00:43:07.705
[MUSIC]

